{
 "war": "wars-of-the-roses",
 "start": "1455-05-20",
 "end": "1487-06-16",
 "note": "There were no fronts in these dynastic wars: England takes the colour of the house holding the crown, campaigns are arrows, and the few areas show only well-attested control (Calais, Ireland, London in 1460, the Lancastrian north in early 1461, the Readeption and Tudor England in late 1485); routes are approximate.",
 "chapters": [
  {
   "id": "st-albans-ludford",
   "title": "From St Albans to Ludford Bridge",
   "start": "1455-05-20",
   "end": "1459-10-13",
   "bbox": [
    -7.2,
    50.6,
    2.3,
    54.6
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "York, Salisbury and Warwick march on St Albans",
    "Henry VI and Somerset march from London to St Albans",
    "Warwick crosses from Calais to join York at Ludlow",
    "Salisbury marches from Middleham by way of Blore Heath to Ludlow",
    "The royal army advances to Ludford Bridge"
   ],
   "text": "Richard, Duke of York and his Neville allies marched on the court of the unstable Henry VI and won a street fight at St Albans on 22 May 1455, killing the Duke of Somerset. Open war returned in 1459: Salisbury beat a larger royal army at Blore Heath, but at Ludford Bridge in October the Yorkist army melted away. York fled to Ireland, where he was the king's lieutenant, and Warwick to Calais, whose garrison stayed loyal to him."
  },
  {
   "id": "northampton-wakefield",
   "title": "Northampton, the Act of Accord and Wakefield",
   "start": "1460-06-26",
   "end": "1460-12-30",
   "bbox": [
    -7.2,
    50.6,
    2.3,
    54.6
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Warwick, Salisbury and March land at Sandwich and march on Northampton",
    "York returns from Ireland to London",
    "York marches north to Sandal Castle"
   ],
   "text": "Warwick, Salisbury and York's son Edward crossed from Calais in late June 1460, were welcomed in London and captured Henry VI at Northampton on 10 July. York came back from Ireland and claimed the throne; Parliament's Act of Accord let Henry reign for life but made York his heir. Queen Margaret's supporters gathered in the north, and York, marching against them, was killed near Wakefield on 30 December."
  },
  {
   "id": "towton",
   "title": "Towton: Edward IV takes the crown",
   "start": "1461-01-01",
   "end": "1461-03-29",
   "bbox": [
    -3.8,
    50.9,
    1.0,
    55.9
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Edward of March marches from Mortimer's Cross to London",
    "Queen Margaret's army marches south to St Albans",
    "The Lancastrian army falls back to York",
    "Edward IV and Warwick march north to Towton"
   ],
   "text": "York's heir Edward won at Mortimer's Cross on 2 February 1461, while Queen Margaret's northern army marched south, beat Warwick at the Second Battle of St Albans and freed Henry VI. London shut its gates to her; Edward entered the city and was proclaimed king on 4 March. He followed the Lancastrians north and crushed them in a snowstorm at Towton on 29 March, perhaps the bloodiest battle ever fought in England."
  },
  {
   "id": "readeption",
   "title": "Warwick turns: Edgcote to the Readeption",
   "start": "1469-07-24",
   "end": "1470-10-06",
   "bbox": [
    -5.8,
    49.9,
    1.9,
    55.0
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Warwick lands at Dartmouth and marches on London",
    "Edward IV flees to King's Lynn and sails for Burgundy"
   ],
   "text": "Warwick, Edward IV's mightiest supporter, fell out with the king and backed risings against him: royal forces were beaten at Edgcote in July 1469, but Edward crushed a Lincolnshire rebellion at Losecoat Field in March 1470 and Warwick fled to France. Reconciled with Queen Margaret, Warwick landed at Dartmouth on 13 September 1470; Edward fled to Burgundy, and on 3 October Henry VI was restored to the throne."
  },
  {
   "id": "barnet-tewkesbury",
   "title": "Barnet and Tewkesbury",
   "start": "1471-03-14",
   "end": "1471-05-21",
   "bbox": [
    -4.0,
    50.4,
    1.0,
    54.4
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Edward IV lands at Ravenspurn and marches on London",
    "Edward IV marches out to Barnet",
    "Queen Margaret marches from Weymouth towards the Severn",
    "Edward IV pursues Margaret to Tewkesbury"
   ],
   "text": "Edward IV landed at Ravenspurn on 14 March 1471 with Burgundian help, gathered men on his way south and took back London and the crown on 11 April. Three days later he killed Warwick at Barnet. Queen Margaret, landing at Weymouth that same day, tried to reach Wales, but Gloucester barred its gates and Edward caught her army at Tewkesbury on 4 May; her son was killed, and Henry VI died in the Tower soon after."
  },
  {
   "id": "bosworth",
   "title": "Bosworth",
   "start": "1485-08-07",
   "end": "1485-08-22",
   "bbox": [
    -5.6,
    51.4,
    -0.6,
    53.3
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Henry Tudor's march from Mill Bay to Bosworth",
    "Richard III marches from Nottingham to Bosworth"
   ],
   "text": "Richard III had taken the throne in 1483 from his nephew, Edward IV's young son. The Lancastrian heir Henry Tudor landed at Mill Bay on Milford Haven on 7 August 1485 with French help, marched through Wales gathering support and crossed into England at Shrewsbury. Richard rode out from Nottingham by way of Leicester, and on 22 August near Market Bosworth he was killed when the Stanleys joined Henry, who became king as Henry VII."
  },
  {
   "id": "stoke",
   "title": "Stoke Field: the last Yorkist challenge",
   "start": "1487-05-04",
   "end": "1487-06-16",
   "bbox": [
    -7.2,
    52.2,
    0.2,
    54.6
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Lincoln and Lambert Simnel sail from Dublin and march to Stoke",
    "Henry VII marches from Kenilworth to Stoke"
   ],
   "text": "Yorkist exiles led by the Earl of Lincoln took the boy pretender Lambert Simnel to Ireland, where the Yorkist cause was popular, and crowned him 'Edward VI' in Dublin on 24 May 1487. They landed in Lancashire on 4 June and marched fast across the Pennines; York held out for Henry VII. On 16 June the king's army destroyed them at East Stoke near Newark, and Simnel was captured."
  }
